{"builder":"Make one pull request that meets every line of the issue's expect list:\n- math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\n- clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\n- README.md documents every function math.js exports\n- npm test passes\n\nSteps:\n1. In math.js, add a function clamp(x, lo, hi) with a short doc comment in the same style as the existing functions. If lo > hi, throw a RangeError with a clear message (e.g. mentioning lo and hi). Otherwise return lo when x < lo, hi when x > hi, and x otherwise (lo and hi themselves are allowed values). Add clamp to the module.exports object next to add, subtract and multiply.\n2. In test/math.test.js, import clamp and add tests using node:test and node:assert/strict like the existing ones: a value below the range returns lo (e.g. clamp(-5, 0, 100) === 0), above returns hi (clamp(140, 0, 100) === 100), inside returns x (clamp(42, 0, 100) === 42), the boundaries return themselves, lo === hi returns that value, and assert.throws(() => clamp(5, 10, 0), RangeError).\n3. In README.md's \"## API\" section, make sure every function exported from math.js has its own entry. Note that subtract is exported but currently NOT documented in the README: add a \"### subtract(a, b)\" entry (what it returns plus a short example), and add a \"### clamp(x, lo, hi)\" entry that says it returns x limited to the range lo to hi, that it throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi, and gives an example (e.g. clamp(140, 0, 100); // 100). Keep the existing add and multiply entries. Follow the existing heading and code-block style.\n4. Run `npm test` from the repository root and make sure it passes. Also run `node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); console.log(Object.keys(m))\"` and confirm each listed name has a README entry.\n5. Commit and open the pull request, describing the change and referencing https://github.com/o/r/issues/23.","verifier":"Run every check at the pull request head, from the repository root. Each command runs on its own.\n\nCheck 1 — expect: \"math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\"\nRun:\nnode -e \"const {clamp}=require('./math.js'); const cases=[[-5,0,100,0],[140,0,100,100],[42,0,100,42],[0,0,100,0],[100,0,100,100],[7,3,3,3],[-20,-10,-1,-10],[5.5,1,5,5]]; let ok=typeof clamp==='function'; for (const [x,lo,hi,want] of cases){const got=clamp(x,lo,hi); if(got!==want){ok=false; console.log('MISMATCH',x,lo,hi,'got',got,'want',want);}} console.log(ok?'OK':'BAD')\"\nPASS: math.js exports a function named clamp and every case prints no MISMATCH (output OK): values below lo give lo, above hi give hi, values in the range (including the ends) give x.\nFAIL: clamp is not exported, is not a function, or any case gives a value other than x limited to lo..hi.\n\nCheck 2 — expect: \"clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\"\nRun:\nnode -e \"const {clamp}=require('./math.js'); const r=[]; for (const [x,lo,hi] of [[5,10,0],[0,1,0],[50,100,-100]]){ try { clamp(x,lo,hi); r.push('no throw'); } catch(e){ r.push(e instanceof RangeError ? 'RangeError' : 'other: '+(e&&e.name)); } } console.log(r.join(','))\"\nPASS: every call with lo greater than hi throws a RangeError (output RangeError,RangeError,RangeError).\nFAIL: any such call returns without throwing, or throws something that is not a RangeError.\n\nCheck 3 — expect: \"README.md documents every function math.js exports\"\nRun: node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); console.log(Object.keys(m).filter(k=>typeof m[k]==='function').join('\\n'))\"\nThen read README.md at the head.\nPASS: for every function name the command prints (expect at least add, subtract, multiply, clamp), README.md contains documentation of that function (an entry that names it and describes what it does).\nFAIL: any exported function, e.g. subtract or clamp, is not documented in README.md.\n\nCheck 4 — expect: \"npm test passes\"\nRun: npm test\nPASS: the test run passes (no failing tests).\nFAIL: any test fails or the test run errors.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail conditions):\n- Check 3: a quick screen is `grep -nE '^#+ .*(add|subtract|multiply|clamp)\\(' README.md`, ideally with entries under the \"## API\" section and the clamp entry mentioning the RangeError; judge by the README's content, not just headings.\n- Check 4: npm test should exit with status 0; it is good if test/math.test.js gained tests for clamp, including the RangeError case.\n- The RangeError message ideally names lo and hi."}